=== WHAT THESE NUMBERS DECIDE ===
A. The one window law is reusable as it stands: _window_start(head, N) answers
   (head, usable, threshold) with threshold = head - (N-1) days, inclusive on
   both ends (P1d/P1e), refuses an unusable head (P1f) and clamps N to 1 (P1g).
   Nothing else in the module windows day rows by DATE: summarize_failure_days
   keeps a COUNT tail (Q1a/Q1b) and counts the whole file (Q1c).
B. The unwindowed header is pinned by TWO exact-equality arms (P2a), so the fix
   may not grow that shape by default. A window therefore has to be OPT-IN, and
   an explicit flag is the only shape that leaves both arms byte-identical
   (P3c: the branch reads no --days today; Q4b: no name collision).
C. days= means "usable rows in the file" and NOTHING in the suite reads it as
   the printed-line count (Q2a/Q2c), so a windowed run can keep days= honest
   and add shown=/hidden= beside it instead of redefining days=.
D. Duplicates are real (P4f) and file order is what the reader yields, so a
   window filters by day only - no sort, no de-duplication, head = the file's
   own newest row (data-derived, no clock).
E. self-correction, disclosed: rev1's P1c looked for rows[-limit:] while the
   code writes rows[-max(1, int(limit)):], and its P3d label was swallowed by a
   broken print string; both were re-measured in rev2 rather than quietly
   dropped.
